      "text": "To solve this problem, we need to calculate the area of the large square and subtract the area of the four equal circles from it.\n\n1. **Calculate the area of the square:**\n   - Given the side length \\( 20 \\) inches.\n   - The area of the square is calculated by the formula \\( \\text{side}^2 \\).\n   \\[\n   \\text{Area of the square} = 20^2 = 400 \\text{ square inches}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Calculate the area of one circle:**\n   - Each circle is tangent to two sides of the square and its two adjacent circles.\n   - The diameter of each circle is equal to the side length of the square divided by \\( \\sqrt{2} \\), since the circles fit perfectly in the space between the top and bottom or the left and right.\n   - Therefore, the diameter of each circle is:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Diameter} = \\frac{20}{\\sqrt{2}} = 10\\sqrt{2}\n   \\]\n   - The radius of each circle is half of the diameter:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Radius} = \\frac{10\\sqrt{2}}{2} = 5\\sqrt{2}\n   \\]\n   - The area of one circle is given by \\( \\pi r^2 \\):\n   \\[\n   \\text{Area of one circle} = \\pi (5\\sqrt{2})^2 = \\pi \\times 50 = 50\\pi \\text{ square inches}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Calculate the total area of the four circles:**\n   - There are four circles, so the total area of the circles is:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Total area of the circles} = 4 \\times 50\\pi = 200\\pi \\text{ square inches}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Calculate the shaded area:**\n   - The shaded area is the area of the square minus the total area of the four circles:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Shaded area} = 400 - 200\\pi\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the area of the shaded region is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{400 - 200\\pi}\n\\]",
